// Adventure: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== adventure.f01 | feature | journey
title: The Eighteenth Man
genre: adventure + sea-adventure
tone: Salt, hunger and stubborn arithmetic; wonder that costs something
era: 1522 | period
setting: The nao Victoria, from the Spice Islands to Sanlucar | real | ship
logline: The ship's boy of the last vessel of a five-ship fleet must keep its dying pilot alive for nine thousand miles, because the pilot is the only one who knows they have lost a day.
premise: Of five ships and some two hundred and seventy men that sailed west for the Spice Islands, one leaking ship is coming home the long way, full of cloves and short of everything else. {tomas}, fourteen, keeps the sandglasses. {kouros}, the pilot, keeps the log, and has noticed that the log and the calendar no longer agree. The captain, {ochandiano}, does not want to hear it.
synopsis: {tomas} turns the half-hour glass and sings the watch; it is the one job on the ship nobody else wants and nobody can do without. Crossing the Indian Ocean far south to avoid the Portuguese, the crew start to die of scurvy at the rate of one a week. {kouros} teaches {tomas} to take the sun with an astrolabe because his own hands shake, and shows him the thing that frightens him: by his reckoning it is Wednesday, and by the count of glasses it is Wednesday, but something in the sums says the world will tell them otherwise. {ochandiano} needs the men to believe in the log; a pilot who says the days are wrong is a pilot who is lost. At the Cape the foremast splits. {mina}, a Malay interpreter taken aboard at Tidore and the best sailor left, refits it with rope she makes from the cargo sacks, and bargains for her passage home with the only thing the ship has, which is cloves. Starving, they put in at the Cape Verde Islands, in Portuguese hands, pretending to come from the Americas. Ashore for rice, {tomas} asks the day and is told Thursday. He has not missed a glass in three years. The lie about where they came from is discovered; thirteen men are seized on the quay; {ochandiano} cuts the cable and runs. Eighteen men bring the ship into Sanlucar. {kouros}, carried ashore, tells the customs clerk the date and is corrected, and laughs until he coughs: they have sailed round the world and it has kept one day for itself. {tomas} walks barefoot to the shrine with a candle, as vowed, and turns the glass in his pocket out of habit.
question: What do you do when you have kept faithful count and the world still says you are wrong?
themes: knowledge that arrives as an error; what a voyage costs those who do the counting; home as a number
audience: Adults and older children who like true voyages
viewpoint: With {tomas}; the sea is seen from the height of a boy on a rail
stakes: Eighteen lives, a cargo worth more than the fleet that fetched it, and a discovery nobody aboard has a word for
conflict: A boy and a dying pilot against hunger, the Portuguese, and a captain who cannot afford a pilot who doubts the calendar
ending: Eighteen come home. The lost day is real. {tomas} keeps turning the glass.
experience: Awe measured in hunger, a fright that turns out to be a discovery, and landfall that hurts to watch
look: A hull patched past belief; sun on empty sea; faces thinning shot by shot; instruments of brass and string
sound: The glass sung every half hour, the pump, worm-eaten timber working. One voice fewer in the watch song each reel.
m: survive | nature | faith-vs-evidence | bittersweet
@ tomas | Tomas de Gamboa | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 14 | To get home having never missed a glass | It is the only thing he has ever been trusted with | The glasses say one day and the land says another | Exact, superstitious, braver than he knows | From keeping time to understanding it can be lost honestly | Basque-accented Castilian, sings his answers | Barefoot, a sandglass on a cord
@ kouros | Andreas Kouros | co-lead | mentor | human | he/him | 47 | To bring home a true log | A pilot is only his reckoning | Scurvy, and a truth his captain forbids | Meticulous and afraid of his own sums | From hiding the error to laughing at it | Dry, Greek-accented, counts under his breath | Swollen hands, an astrolabe he can no longer hold steady
@ ochandiano | Martin de Ochandiano | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 36 | To arrive, with the cargo | He was not the fleet's first captain, nor its third | He needs certainty he does not have | Brave, hard, right about most things | From silencing the pilot to carrying him ashore | Short orders, long silences | Salt-cracked boots, the fleet's last good coat
@ mina | Mina of Tidore | supporting | ally | human | she/her | 28 | Passage back east, paid in advance | She was taken as an interpreter and means to leave as a creditor | She is the best sailor aboard and the least believed | Practical, amused, owes these men nothing | From cargo to the one who refits the mast | Four languages, each used to bargain | Sarong, a sailmaker's palm, a bag of cloves she counts nightly
~ tomas - kouros | ship's boy and pilot | a boy who holds the instrument for him | a witness to the lost day
~ kouros - ochandiano | pilot and captain | a truth forbidden | a captain carrying his pilot up a quay
~ tomas - mina | the two youngest hands aboard | wary | she teaches him the knot that holds the mast
# The Indian Ocean | EXT | Victoria, Quarterdeck | NIGHT | tomas, kouros | Keep the count | {tomas} turns the glass and sings the watch; {kouros} checks the log by lantern and frowns at a sum that will not close | | 4 | water, props=sandglass+logbook+lantern
# The Indian Ocean | EXT | Victoria, Waist | DAY | tomas, kouros, ochandiano | Forbid the doubt | A burial, the fourth this month. {kouros} tells {ochandiano} the calendar may be out. He is told to keep it out of the book | {tomas} is taught the astrolabe in secret | 5 | water, crowd=20, props=astrolabe+shroud
# The Cape | EXT | Victoria, Foredeck | DAY | mina, tomas, ochandiano | Save the mast | The foremast splits in a gale. {mina} fishes it with rope made from clove sacks and names her price for doing so | {mina} becomes the ship's creditor | 6 | water, stunts=storm rigging work, vfx=gale seas, props=rope+broken spar
# Cape Verde | EXT | Ribeira Grande, Quay | DAY | tomas, mina | Ask the day | Ashore for rice with a false story. {tomas} asks a boy what day it is. Thursday. He counts on his fingers and counts again | The lost day is real | 5 | crowd=40, props=rice sacks
# Cape Verde | EXT | Victoria, Deck | DUSK | ochandiano, kouros, tomas, mina | Run | The lie is out. Thirteen men are taken on the quay. {ochandiano} cuts the cable with his own hand | Thirteen are left behind | 5 | water, crowd=30, stunts=cable cut under fire
# Sanlucar | EXT | Sanlucar, River Landing | DAY | kouros, ochandiano, tomas | Come home | Eighteen men, a ship that should not float. A clerk corrects {kouros}'s date, and the pilot laughs until he coughs | | 5 | water, crowd=60
# Sanlucar | EXT | Road To The Shrine | DAY | tomas, mina | Keep the vow | Barefoot, candle in hand. {mina} walks the other way, toward the ships. In his pocket, {tomas} turns the glass | | 3 | props=candle+sandglass
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the first circumnavigation's return, with invented people
